-best_neg_tokens\n    selected_indices = list(best_indices)\n    total_score = best_score\n    return {\n        \"selected_indices\": selected_indices,\n        \"tokens_used\": tokens_used,\n        \"total_score\": total_score\n    }\n\nCheck tie-breaking: For same score, we want fewer tokens. Our tuple comparison: (score, -tokens, indices). Higher -tokens = lower tokens, so that compares correctly. For same score and same tokens, lower indices tuple is better (since smaller first index). That's fine.\n\nNow we need to output as JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source code as a string. Escape properly.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lengths = case['lengths']\\n    scores = case['scores']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(lengths)\\n    dp = [(0, 0, ()) for _ in range(budget+1)]\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        l = lengths[i]\\n        s = scores[i]\\n        for cap in range(budget, l-1, -1):\\n            prev_score, prev_neg_tokens, prev_indices = dp[cap - l]\\n            new_score = prev_score + s\\n            new_neg_tokens = prev_neg_tokens - l\\n            new_indices = prev_indices + (i,)\\n            new_tuple = (new_score, new_neg_tokens, new_indices)\\n            if new_tuple > dp[cap]:\\n                dp[cap] = new_tuple\\n    best = (0, 0, ())\\n    for cap in range(budget+1):\\n        if dp[cap] > best:\\n            best = dp[cap]\\n    best_score, best_neg_tokens, best_indices = best\\n    tokens_used = -best_neg_tokens\\n    selected_indices = list(best_indices)\\n    total_score = best_score\\n    return {\\n        \\\"selected_indices\\\": selected_indices,\\n        \\\"tokens_used\\\": tokens_used,\\n        \\\"total_score\\\": total_score\\n    }\"}\n\nMake sure to escape double quotes and backslashes. In the string, we have double quotes around dict keys, so we need to escape them. Also newlines are fine as \\n.\n\nLet's double-check: The string should be a valid